{"type": "spr", "stem": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A circle has center <math alttext=\"upper O\"><mi>O</mi>\n</math>, and points <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper U\"><mi>U</mi>\n</math> lie on the circle. Line segments <math alttext=\"upper A upper E\"><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></math> and <math alttext=\"upper U upper E\"><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi></math> are tangent to the circle at points <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper U\"><mi>U</mi>\n</math>, respectively. The distance between point <math alttext=\"upper O\"><mi>O</mi>\n</math> and point <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"510\"><mn>510</mn>\n</math> centimeters and the distance between point <math alttext=\"upper O\"><mi>O</mi>\n</math> and point <math alttext=\"upper U\"><mi>U</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"78\"><mn>78</mn>\n</math> centimeters. What is the area, in square centimeters, of quadrilateral <math alttext=\"upper O upper A upper E upper U\"><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>U</mi></math>?</p>", "keys": ["39312"], "rationale": "<p>The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"39,312\"><mn>39,312</mn></math>. It's given that the circle has center <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O\"><mi>O</mi></math>&nbsp;and points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper U\"><mi>U</mi></math> lie on the circle. It follows that segments <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper A\"><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper U\"><mi>O</mi><mi>U</mi></math> are radii of the circle and therefore congruent. It's also given that line segments <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper E\"><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper U upper E\"><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;are tangent to the circle at points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper U\"><mi>U</mi></math>, respectively. Since line segments <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper E\"><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper U upper E\"><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;are tangent to the circle from a common external point, point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi></math>, line segments <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper E\"><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper U upper E\"><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;are congruent and form right angles with radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper A\"><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper U\"><mi>O</mi><mi>U</mi></math>, respectively. Therefore, quadrilateral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper A upper E upper U\"><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>U</mi></math>&nbsp;consists of two congruent right triangles: triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper A upper E\"><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;and triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper U upper E\"><mi>O</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi></math>. It's given that the distance between point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O\"><mi>O</mi></math>&nbsp;and point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"510\"><mn>510</mn></math>&nbsp;centimeters and the distance between point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O\"><mi>O</mi></math>&nbsp;and point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper U\"><mi>U</mi></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"78\"><mn>78</mn></math>&nbsp;centimeters. It follows that the area of triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper U upper E\"><mi>O</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;can be found by first using the Pythagorean theorem to calculate the distance between point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper U\"><mi>U</mi></math>&nbsp;and point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi></math>. For right triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper U upper E\"><mi>O</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi></math>, where angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper U\"><mi>U</mi></math>&nbsp;is a right angle, the Pythagorean theorem states that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis upper O upper U right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis upper U upper E right parenthesis squared equals left parenthesis upper O upper E right parenthesis squared\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>O</mi><mi>U</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>O</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"78\"><mn>78</mn></math>&nbsp;for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper U\"><mi>O</mi><mi>U</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"510\"><mn>510</mn></math>&nbsp;for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper E\"><mi>O</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;in this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"78 squared plus left parenthesis upper U upper E right parenthesis squared equals 510 squared\"><msup><mn>78</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>510</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6,084 plus left parenthesis upper U upper E right parenthesis squared equals 260,100\"><mn>6,084</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>260,100</mn></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6,084\"><mn>6,084</mn></math>&nbsp;from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis upper U upper E right parenthesis squared equals 254,016\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>254,016</mn></math>. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper U upper E equals 504\"><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>504</mn></math>. Thus, triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper U upper E\"><mi>O</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;is a right triangle with legs of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"78\"><mn>78</mn></math>&nbsp;centimeters and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"504\"><mn>504</mn></math>&nbsp;centimeters. It follows that the area of triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper U upper E\"><mi>O</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis one half right parenthesis left parenthesis 78 right parenthesis left parenthesis 504 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>78</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>504</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;square centimeters, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"19,656\"><mn>19,656</mn></math>&nbsp;square centimeters. Since triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper A upper E\"><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;is congruent to triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper U upper E\"><mi>O</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>E</mi></math>, the area of triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper A upper E\"><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"19,656\"><mn>19,656</mn></math>&nbsp;square centimeters. Therefore, the area of quadrilateral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper O upper A upper E upper U\"><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>U</mi></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"19,656 plus 19,656\"><mn>19,656</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>19,656</mn></math>&nbsp;square centimeters, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"39,312\"><mn>39,312</mn></math>&nbsp;square centimeters.</p>", "externalid": "19517d3f-5560-4607-a8d2-e73bd6c13c56", "correct_answer": ["39312"]}